---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const base = `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '')}/`;
const href = (route: string) => `${base}${route.replace(/^\//, '')}`;
---

<BaseLayout title="Reference" description="Reviewed courses, independent explanations, and interactive repositories for electronic-structure study." current="reference">
  <h1>Reference</h1>
  <p class="lede">A useful resource does not become trustworthy because it carries a famous university name, and an independent course does not become weak because it was made by a small team. Selection here is based on authorship, structure, technical fit, access, maintenance, and a specific learning role.</p>
  <p>These routes are alternatives, not a ranking. Use the resource that matches the gap: a complete semester course, a compact Chinese review, a conceptual bridge, or an executable notebook. None of them replaces the scope and evidence boundaries stated on the corresponding Theory page.</p>

  <h2>Chinese-language university courses</h2>
  <p><a href="https://www.bilibili.com/video/BV1ki4y1A7Mh/"><cite lang="zh-CN">大学物理《电磁学》</cite>, University of Science and Technology of China School of the Gifted Young</a> is a 116-lecture recording published by the school's official Bilibili account. It is the most complete Chinese route in this first review for <a href={href('/theory/electromagnetism/')}>Electromagnetism</a>, including electrostatics, dielectric media, boundary-value methods, magnetism, induction, gauge ideas, Maxwell equations, and waves. Its length makes it a course rather than a quick repair resource.</p>
  <p><a href="https://www.icourse163.org/course/NUDT-1206139804"><cite lang="zh-CN">简明固体物理</cite>, National University of Defense Technology</a> is a national-quality Chinese University MOOC covering crystal structure, lattice vibrations, free electrons, and band theory in about ten hours. It is a lower-barrier route into <a href={href('/theory/solid-state-physics/')}>Solid-State Physics</a>; its compact design does not replace an advanced treatment of many-body response, magnetism, or topology.</p>

  <h2>Independent Bilibili courses</h2>
  <p><a href="https://www.bilibili.com/video/BV1yh4y1A7ja/"><cite lang="zh-CN">B站物院の量子力学</cite></a> is a self-produced compact sequence spanning wavefunctions, the Schrödinger equation, model potentials, operators, Hilbert-space language, representations, hydrogen, spin, angular momentum, and identical particles. It is useful for a Chinese first pass or structured review of <a href={href('/theory/quantum-mechanics/')}>Quantum Mechanics</a>. The short chapters should be paired with a textbook and substantial problem solving.</p>
  <p><a href="https://www.bilibili.com/video/BV1Hs4y1n7T8/"><cite lang="zh-CN">B站物院の固体物理</cite></a> is a concise independent series with paired chapter exercises on crystal structure, reciprocal lattices and diffraction, bonding, lattice vibrations, phonon thermodynamics, the free-electron gas, bands, and semiconductors. It is a strong revision route, but its compressed format is not a substitute for a full proof- and derivation-level solid-state course.</p>
  <p><a href="https://www.bilibili.com/video/BV1Kk4y1e7He/"><cite lang="zh-CN">普通物理学—电磁学</cite> by 大T-Peachsea</a> is a completed handwritten sequence with detailed derivations and examples across electrostatics, steady currents, magnetostatics, induction, media, circuits, Maxwell theory, and electromagnetic waves. It is valuable for learners who need to follow calculations line by line. The creator describes the work as an independent undergraduate project, so it should be used as a supplementary explanation rather than the sole authority.</p>
  <p><a href="https://www.bilibili.com/video/BV1spj4zgEv3/"><cite lang="zh-CN">密度泛函理论（2025年5月）</cite> by 孙老师讲物理</a> is a six-part Chinese sequence connecting Thomas–Fermi, Thomas–Fermi–Dirac, Hohenberg–Kohn variation, Kohn–Sham equations, and the local-density approximation. It supplies a coherent narrative bridge into <a href={href('/theory/density-functional-theory-foundations/')}>DFT Foundations</a>. Because it intentionally crosses into Kohn–Sham construction and LDA, the Atlas keeps those responsibilities separated even when the course presents them together.</p>

  <h2>Zhihu conceptual bridges</h2>
  <p><a href="https://www.zhihu.com/tardis/zm/art/169805550"><cite lang="zh-CN">从线性代数到量子力学</cite> by PeiLingX</a> uses spin experiments, basis changes, probability distributions, Fourier transforms, and uncertainty to connect familiar linear algebra with quantum notation. The linked lesson is an entry inside a longer series and works best for conceptual repair between <a href={href('/theory/linear-algebra/')}>Linear Algebra</a> and Quantum Mechanics. It is a guided explanation, not a complete university course or a replacement for exercise-based study.</p>

  <h2>Interactive GitHub routes</h2>
  <p><a href="https://github.com/tjz21/DFT_PIB_Code"><code>tjz21/DFT_PIB_Code</code></a> provides MIT-licensed Jupyter notebooks, a worksheet, Colab access, and an offline route for opening the DFT black box. Its particle-in-a-box and molecular examples expose the Kohn–Sham potential, LDA/PBE choices, density updates, eigenvalues, and SCF iteration. It is the most beginner-friendly executable bridge in this first review, but the teaching model is not evidence for production-material accuracy.</p>
  <p><a href="https://github.com/JuliaMolSim/DFTK.jl"><code>JuliaMolSim/DFTK.jl</code></a> is an actively maintained MIT-licensed plane-wave DFT toolkit designed for numerical and algorithmic transparency. Its tutorials separate the physical model from cutoffs, k-point sampling, and SCF solution, making it valuable after the Kohn–Sham, discretization, plane-wave, and SCF pages. It requires Julia and substantially more numerical maturity than the notebook route.</p>
  <p><a href="https://github.com/JSchoeberl/iFEM"><code>JSchoeberl/iFEM</code></a> is an interactive Jupyter-book course on finite-element theory and computation from TU Wien contributors. It is not an electronic-structure course, but it gives a rigorous experimental bridge from weak formulations and Sobolev spaces to real-space discretization. Use it with <a href={href('/theory/differential-equations/')}>Differential Equations</a>, <a href={href('/theory/functional-analysis-and-variational-methods/')}>Functional Analysis</a>, and numerical representation pages.</p>

  <h2>What this review did not accept</h2>
  <p>Third-party university-course reuploads, “high-definition restoration” copies, textbook-download bundles, marketing-first training pages, exam-cram fragments, and repositories with unclear provenance or unresolved copyright boundaries were not admitted. Popularity, follower count, and GitHub stars were used only as discovery signals, never as scientific review.</p>
  <p>Commercial resources are not excluded merely because they cost money, but a sales page without inspectable syllabus, authorship, technical scope, and access boundaries is not enough. Software documentation is also not treated as scientific validation.</p>

  <h2>Maintenance boundary</h2>
  <p>This is the first diversified resource pass, not an exhaustive catalogue of Chinese-language education. Platform availability, regional access, playlists, repository releases, and creator status can change. Resources should be rechecked periodically and removed when the intended source, authorship, or access boundary can no longer be verified.</p>

  <aside class="review-note">
    <p><strong>Review note.</strong> The first Chinese-platform and independent-repository review was completed on 3 August 2026. Institutional prestige was not used as a quality proxy; each accepted resource has a distinct role and an explicit limitation.</p>
  </aside>
</BaseLayout>
